{:edb/committed true :edb/basis-t 2647 :edb/replayed false :edb/tx-hash "bc48482061c7b6087f0890c8373dbc07f94d1df6497a218badee8c3f1e6108a6" :edb/db-before-t 2646 :edb/db-after-t 2647 :edb/tx-data [[13194139535959 50 "2026-10-15T12:04:48.822Z" 13194139535959 17592186045417 true] [17592186065847 1078 17592186065849 13194139535959 17592186045417 true] [17592186068549 1071 "![](images/18/1867a80955ae4b456cbcb011eefed96431f2b12926994942613eb947e55e38c6.png)\n\n \n\nThe graph of $y=f(x)$ is shown above. Which of the following statements are true? \n\n- $f(x)$ is defined at $x=2$\n\n- $\\lim_\\limits{x\\rightarrow \\, 2}f(x)$ exists\n\n- $f(x)$ is continuous at $x=2$" 13194139535959 17592186045417 false] [17592186068549 1071 "![](images/18/1867a80955ae4b456cbcb011eefed96431f2b12926994942613eb947e55e38c6.png)\n\n \n\nThe graph of $y=f(x)$ is shown above. Which of the following statements are true? \n\nI. $f(x)$ is defined at $x=2$\n\nII. $\\lim_\\limits{x\\rightarrow \\, 2}f(x)$ exists\n\nIII. $f(x)$ is continuous at $x=2$" 13194139535959 17592186045417 true] [17592186068695 1038 17592186548982 13194139535959 17592186045417 true] [17592186068695 1038 17592186549055 13194139535959 17592186045417 true] [17592186068696 1038 17592186548989 13194139535959 17592186045417 true] [17592186068833 1038 17592186549017 13194139535959 17592186045417 true] [17592186068833 1038 17592186549024 13194139535959 17592186045417 true] [17592186068834 1038 17592186548997 13194139535959 17592186045417 true] [17592186068989 1050 420.0 13194139535959 17592186045417 true] [17592186069069 1070 17592186045485 13194139535959 17592186045417 true] [17592186069069 1071 "Consider the quadratic equation $x^{2} + 2x - 15 = 0$. Which of the following statements are true?\n\n1. The discriminant of the equation is a perfect square.\n2. The equation has only rational solutions.\n3. The equation has only irrational solutions." 13194139535959 17592186045417 false] [17592186069069 1071 "Consider the quadratic equation ${x}^{2}+2x-15=0.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. The discriminant of the equation is a perfect square.\n\nII. The equation has only rational solutions.\n\nIII. The equation has only irrational solutions." 13194139535959 17592186045417 true] [17592186069069 1074 "Let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. We have $a=1,$ $b=2,$ and $c=-15.$ So, we calculate the discriminant as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}D & ={b}^{2}-4ac \\\\  & =(2)^{2}-4(1)(-15) \\\\  & =4+60 \\\\  & =64\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAnd $64={8}^{2}$ is a perfect square.\n\n- Statement II is true, while statement III is false. \n\nFirst, notice that our quadratic equation has rational coefficients. And, since the discriminant is a nonzero perfect square, the equation must have two rational solutions.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\"" 13194139535959 17592186045417 true] [17592186069958 1064 "A function $f(x)$ is **continuous** over its entire domain if the graph of $y=f(x)$ can be drawn using a pencil and a piece of paper without taking the pencil off of the paper.\n\nTo illustrate, let's consider the function $y=x^2$ whose graph is shown below. \n\n![](images/67/67ffd0b78845693b522100f4f0ba460cae6fedd796964b24708e320eab04ce29.png)\n\n \n\nWe can draw as much of the function $y=x^2$ as we want without taking our pencil off of the paper. Therefore, we say that $y=x^2$ is **continuous** at every point in its domain.\n\nLet's compare this to the function $y=g(x)$ below:\n\n![](images/a0/a07ccd15d14fa3eeb00a9c4ab07674c1c169e2a0d0a3e9fcb64798e9929e2ff9.png)\n\n \n\nNotice that this function has a jump in its value at $x=0.$\n\nIt is *impossible* to draw $y=g(x)$ without taking the pencil off the paper to fill in the jump at $x=0.$ Therefore, we say that $y=g(x)$ has a **discontinuity** at $x=0.$" 13194139535959 17592186045417 true] [17592186069958 1064 "A function $f(x)$ is **continuous** over its entire domain if the graph of $y=f(x)$ can be drawn using a pencil and a piece of paper without taking the pencil off of the paper.\n\nTo illustrate, let's consider the function $y=x^2$ whose graph is shown below. \n\n![](images/67/67ffd0b78845693b522100f4f0ba460cae6fedd796964b24708e320eab04ce29.png)\n\nWe can draw as much of the function $y=x^2$ as we want without taking our pencil off of the paper. Therefore, we say that $y=x^2$ is **continuous** at every point in its domain.\n\nLet's compare this to the function $y=g(x)$ below:\n\n![](images/a0/a07ccd15d14fa3eeb00a9c4ab07674c1c169e2a0d0a3e9fcb64798e9929e2ff9.png)\n\nNotice that this function has a jump in its value at $x=0.$\n\nIt is *impossible* to draw $y=g(x)$ without taking the pencil off the paper to fill in the jump at $x=0.$ Therefore, we say that $y=g(x)$ has a **discontinuity** at $x=0.$" 13194139535959 17592186045417 false] [17592186069961 1064 "Now consider the function $y=h(x)$ below. This function has a discontinuity at $x=0,$ so it is not continuous over its entire domain.\n\n![](images/4e/4e41ca41e4befba33c62da68666a8c4a2e2c7a19c79331360ae4bf124edafd50.png)\n\n \n\nHowever, the function $h(x)$ may be continuous at other points, such as $x=1.$ \n\nTo determine whether $h(x)$ is continuous at $x=1,$ we have to consider points in the **neighborhood** of $x=1.$ Roughly speaking, the *neighborhood of $x=1$* means the points on the curve that have an $x$-coordinate that's close to $1.$\n\nIndeed, in the *neighborhood* of $x=1,$ we can draw the function $h(x)$ without taking the pencil off the paper. So, $h(x)$ is indeed continuous at $x=1.$" 13194139535959 17592186045417 true] [17592186069961 1064 "Now consider the function $y=h(x)$ below. This function has a discontinuity at $x=0,$ so it is not continuous over its entire domain.\n\n![](images/4e/4e41ca41e4befba33c62da68666a8c4a2e2c7a19c79331360ae4bf124edafd50.png)\n\nHowever, the function $h(x)$ may be continuous at other points, such as $x=1.$ \n\nTo determine whether $h(x)$ is continuous at $x=1,$ we have to consider points in the **neighborhood** of $x=1.$ Roughly speaking, the *neighborhood of $x=1$* means the points on the curve that have an $x$-coordinate that's close to $1.$\n\nIndeed, in the *neighborhood* of $x=1,$ we can draw the function $h(x)$ without taking the pencil off the paper. So, $h(x)$ is indeed continuous at $x=1.$" 13194139535959 17592186045417 false] [17592186071372 1078 17592186071377 13194139535959 17592186045417 true] [17592186073878 1038 17592186549015 13194139535959 17592186045417 true] [17592186073878 1038 17592186549029 13194139535959 17592186045417 true] [17592186073878 1038 17592186549051 13194139535959 17592186045417 true] [17592186074189 1070 17592186045485 13194139535959 17592186045417 true] [17592186074189 1071 "![](images/2c/2c9f6bba4d2a0e1b1499a8813a2035f838113cbcb5e0fe20c219f45bf011c471.png)\n\nSolve for $x$ in the figure above given that $PQ^(⟷)$ is the perpendicular bisector of $MN^(―)$." 13194139535959 17592186045417 false] [17592186074189 1071 "![](images/2c/2c9f6bba4d2a0e1b1499a8813a2035f838113cbcb5e0fe20c219f45bf011c471.png)\n\nSolve for $x$ in the figure above given that $\\overset{⟷}{PQ}$ is the perpendicular bisector of $\\overset{―}{MN}.$" 13194139535959 17592186045417 true] [17592186074189 1074 "By the perpendicular bisector theorem, a point on the perpendicular bisector of a segment is equidistant from the endpoints of the segment. Hence, we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}QM & =QN \\\\ 2x-3 & =3x-10 \\\\ -3 & =x-10 \\\\ 7 & =x\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535959 17592186045417 true] [17592186078092 1075 17592186078093 13194139535959 17592186045417 false] [17592186078092 1075 17592186078094 13194139535959 17592186045417 false] [17592186078092 1075 17592186078095 13194139535959 17592186045417 false] [17592186078092 1075 17592186078096 13194139535959 17592186045417 false] [17592186078092 1075 17592186078097 13194139535959 17592186045417 false] [17592186078092 1075 17592186548396 13194139535959 17592186045417 true] [17592186078092 1075 17592186548472 13194139535959 17592186045417 true] [17592186078092 1075 17592186548743 13194139535959 17592186045417 true] [17592186078092 1075 17592186548803 13194139535959 17592186045417 true] [17592186078092 1075 17592186548857 13194139535959 17592186045417 true] [17592186078092 1078 17592186548396 13194139535959 17592186045417 true] [17592186078510 1038 17592186549005 13194139535959 17592186045417 true] [17592186078510 1038 17592186549039 13194139535959 17592186045417 true] [17592186079219 1070 17592186045487 13194139535959 17592186045417 true] [17592186079219 1071 "The graph of $y = 4f(2x) - 3$, can be obtained from the graph of $y = f(x)$ by applying **some** of the following transformations:\n\n- $(T1)$ - Translate up by $3$ units\n- $(T2)$ - Stretch by a scale factor of $4$ parallel to the $y$-axis\n- $(T3)$ - Translate down by $3$ units\n- $(T4)$ - Stretch by a scale factor of $0.5$ parallel to the $x$-axis\n\nWhich of the following could be a possible ordering of the appropriate transformations?" 13194139535959 17592186045417 false] [17592186079219 1071 "The graph of $y=4f(2x)-3,$ can be obtained from the graph of $y=f(x)$ by applying **some** of the following transformations:\n\n- $(\\text{T}1)$ - Translate up by $3$ units\n\n- $(\\text{T}2)$ - Stretch by a scale factor of $4$ parallel to the $y$-axis\n\n- $(\\text{T}3)$ - Translate down by $3$ units\n\n- $(\\text{T}4)$ - Stretch by a scale factor of $0.5$ parallel to the $x$-axis\n\nWhich of the following could be a possible ordering of the appropriate transformations?" 13194139535959 17592186045417 true] [17592186079219 1074 "To perform transformations of functions with multiple operations, we must remember the following rule:\n\n1\\. First, we perform the transformations *inside* the argument of $f.$ We first carry out horizontal stretches, followed by horizontal translations.\n\n2\\. Then, we perform the transformations *outside* the argument of $f.$ We first carry out vertical stretches, followed by vertical translations.\n\nTo get $y=4f(2x)-3,$ we could apply the following steps:\n\n- Take the curve $y=f(x).$\n\n- Apply transformation $(\\text{T}4).$ Stretch $y=f(x)$ by a scale factor of $\\frac{1}{2}=0.5$ parallel to the $x$-axis to get the graph of \n\n$$\ny=f(2x).\n$$\n\n- Apply transformation $(\\text{T}2).$ Stretch $y=f(2x)$ by a scale factor of $4$ parallel to the $y$-axis to get the graph of \n\n$$\ny=4f(2x).\n$$\n\n- Apply transformation $(\\text{T}3).$ Translate $y=4f(2x)$ down by $3$ units to get the graph of \n\n$$\ny=4f(2x)-3.\n$$\n\nTherefore, a possible order of transformations is\n\n$\\,$ $(\\text{T}4)\\to (\\text{T}2)\\to (\\text{T}3).$" 13194139535959 17592186045417 true] [17592186089531 1075 17592186089532 13194139535959 17592186045417 false] [17592186089531 1075 17592186089533 13194139535959 17592186045417 false] [17592186089531 1075 17592186089534 13194139535959 17592186045417 false] [17592186089531 1075 17592186089535 13194139535959 17592186045417 false] [17592186089531 1075 17592186089536 13194139535959 17592186045417 false] [17592186089531 1075 17592186548372 13194139535959 17592186045417 true] [17592186089531 1075 17592186548421 13194139535959 17592186045417 true] [17592186089531 1075 17592186548444 13194139535959 17592186045417 true] [17592186089531 1075 17592186548660 13194139535959 17592186045417 true] [17592186089531 1075 17592186548783 13194139535959 17592186045417 true] [17592186089531 1078 17592186548421 13194139535959 17592186045417 true] [17592186089645 1075 17592186089646 13194139535959 17592186045417 false] [17592186089645 1075 17592186089647 13194139535959 17592186045417 false] [17592186089645 1075 17592186089648 13194139535959 17592186045417 false] [17592186089645 1075 17592186089649 13194139535959 17592186045417 false] [17592186089645 1075 17592186089650 13194139535959 17592186045417 false] [17592186089645 1075 17592186548384 13194139535959 17592186045417 true] [17592186089645 1075 17592186548520 13194139535959 17592186045417 true] [17592186089645 1075 17592186548609 13194139535959 17592186045417 true] [17592186089645 1075 17592186548726 13194139535959 17592186045417 true] [17592186089645 1075 17592186548862 13194139535959 17592186045417 true] [17592186089645 1078 17592186548520 13194139535959 17592186045417 true] [17592186089651 1078 17592186089655 13194139535959 17592186045417 true] [17592186089657 1078 17592186089659 13194139535959 17592186045417 true] [17592186091934 1071 "Let $\\mathbf{X} = [X_1, X_2]^T$ be a bivariate normal random vector. The variances of $X_1$ and $X_2$ and their correlation coefficient are given by\n\n$$\n\\sigma_1^2=9,\\qquad \\sigma_2^2 = 1, \\qquad \\rho = \\dfrac1{3}.\n$$\n\nGiven that the joint probability density function of $\\mathbf X$ is\n\n$$\nf(\\mathbf x) = \\dfrac1k\\,\\mathrm{exp}\\left( -\\dfrac 12(\\mathbf x - \\boldsymbol \\mu)^T \\,\\Sigma^{-1} \\,(\\mathbf x - \\boldsymbol \\mu)\\right),\n$$\n\nwhere $\\boldsymbol{\\mu}$ is the mean vector and $\\Sigma$ is the corresponding covariance matrix, find the value of $k.$" 13194139535959 17592186045417 true] [17592186091934 1071 "Let $\\mathbf{X} = [X_1, X_2]^T$ be a bivariate normal random vector. The variances of $X_1$ and $X_2$ and their correlation coefficient are given by\n\n$\\sigma_1^2=9,\\qquad \\sigma_2^2 = 1, \\qquad \\rho = \\dfrac1{3}$.\n\nGiven that the joint probability density function of $\\mathbf X$ is\n$f(\\mathbf x) = \\dfrac1k\\,\\mathrm{exp}\\left(-\\dfrac 12(\\mathbf x - \\boldsymbol \\mu)^T \\,\\Sigma^{-1} \\,(\\mathbf x - \\boldsymbol \\mu)\\right)$,\n\nwhere $\\boldsymbol{\\mu}$ is the mean vector and $\\Sigma$ is the corresponding covariance matrix, find the value of $k$." 13194139535959 17592186045417 false] [17592186091934 1074 "Recall that if\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{X} = [X_1, X_2]^T\n$$\n\nis a bivariate normal vector with mean vector $\\boldsymbol{\\mu}$ and covariance matrix $\\Sigma$, given by\n\n$$\nμ = (μ_{1}; μ_{2}), Σ = \\begin{bmatrix}σ_{1}^{2} & σ_{12} \\\\ σ_{21} & σ_{2}^{2}\\end{bmatrix} = \\begin{bmatrix}σ_{1}^{2} & ρσ_{1}σ_{2} \\\\ ρσ_{1}σ_{2} & σ_{2}^{2}\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n$$\n\\sigma^2_i = \\textrm{Var}[X_i]\n$$\n\n$$\n\\sigma_{12} = \\textrm{Cov}[X_1,X_2]\n$$\n\nand $\\rho$ is the correlation, then the joint probability density function of $\\mathbf X$ is\n\n$$\nf(\\mathbf x) = \\dfrac{1}{2\\pi\\sqrt{\\mid \\Sigma \\mid}} \\mathrm{exp}\\left(-\\dfrac 12(\\mathbf x - \\boldsymbol \\mu)^T \\,\\Sigma^{-1} \\,(\\mathbf x - \\boldsymbol \\mu)\\right)\n$$\n\nand $\\mid \\Sigma \\mid$ denotes the determinant of $\\Sigma$.\n\nUsing the given values of $\\sigma^2_1, \\sigma^2_2$, and $\\rho$, our covariance matrix is\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}9 & \\frac{1}{3} \\cdot \\sqrt{9} \\cdot \\sqrt{1} \\\\ \\frac{1}{3} \\cdot \\sqrt{9} \\cdot \\sqrt{1} & 1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\mid \\Sigma \\mid = 9\\cdot 1 - 1\\cdot 1 = 8\n$$\n\nSubstituting this value into our expression for the joint PDF, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf(x) &= \\frac{1}{2π\\sqrt{8}}\\exp(-\\frac{1}{2}(x - μ)^{T}Σ^{-1}(x - μ)) \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{4 π 2}\\exp(-\\frac{1}{2}(x - μ)^{T}Σ^{-1}(x - μ))\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\nk=4\\pi\\sqrt 2\n$$" 13194139535959 17592186045417 false] [17592186091934 1074 "Recall that if $\\mathbf{X} = [X_1, X_2]^T$ is a bivariate normal vector with mean vector $\\boldsymbol{\\mu}$ and covariance matrix $\\Sigma,$ given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mu =(\\begin{aligned}{\\mu }_{1} \\\\ {\\mu }_{2}\\end{aligned}),\\,Σ=[\\begin{aligned}{\\sigma }_{1}^{2} & {\\sigma }_{12} \\\\ {\\sigma }_{21} & {\\sigma }_{2}^{2}\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}{\\sigma }_{1}^{2} & \\rho {\\sigma }_{1}{\\sigma }_{2} \\\\ \\rho {\\sigma }_{1}{\\sigma }_{2} & {\\sigma }_{2}^{2}\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $\\sigma^2_i = \\textrm{Var}[X_i],$ $\\sigma_{12} = \\textrm{Cov}[X_1,X_2],$ and $\\rho$ is the correlation, then the joint probability density function of $\\mathbf X$ is\n\n$$\nf(\\mathbf x) = \\dfrac{1}{2\\pi\\sqrt{|\\Sigma|}} \\mathrm{exp}\\left( -\\dfrac 12(\\mathbf x - \\boldsymbol \\mu)^T \\,\\Sigma^{-1} \\,(\\mathbf x - \\boldsymbol \\mu)\\right)\n$$\n\nand $|\\Sigma|$ denotes the determinant of $\\Sigma.$\n\nUsing the given values of $\\sigma^2_1, \\sigma^2_2$, and $\\rho,$ our covariance matrix is\n\n![](images/f4/f44326b7be98378285978658be508da822c110d5d61e8950872cc75c4296d7ae.svg)\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n|\\Sigma| = 9\\cdot 1 - 1\\cdot 1 = 8.\n$$\n\nSubstituting this value into our expression for the joint PDF, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\frac{1}{2\\pi \\sqrt{8}}exp(-\\frac{1}{2}(x-\\mu )^{T}\\,{Σ}^{-1}\\,(x-\\mu )) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4\\pi \\sqrt{2}}exp(-\\frac{1}{2}(x-\\mu )^{T}\\,{Σ}^{-1}\\,(x-\\mu )).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, $k=4\\pi\\sqrt 2.$" 13194139535959 17592186045417 true] [17592186091935 1071 "Let $\\mathbf{X} = [X_1,X_2]^T$ be a bivariate normal random vector with mean vector $\\boldsymbol{\\mu}$ and covariance matrix $\\Sigma$ given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mu =[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}],\\,Σ=[\\begin{aligned}4 & -3 \\\\ -3 & 7\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFind the distribution of $Y = 3X_1 + X_2 .$" 13194139535959 17592186045417 true] [17592186091935 1071 "Let $\\mathbf{X} = [X_1,X_2]^T$ be a bivariate normal random vector with mean vector $\\boldsymbol{\\mu}$ and covariance matrix $\\Sigma$ given by\n$μ = \\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}, Σ = \\begin{bmatrix}4 & -3 \\\\ -3 & 7\\end{bmatrix}$.\nFind the distribution of $Y = 3X_1 + X_2$." 13194139535959 17592186045417 false] [17592186091935 1074 "Recall that if\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{X} = [X_1, X_2]^T\n$$\n\nis a bivariate normal random vector with mean vector $\\boldsymbol{\\mu}$ and covariance matrix $\\Sigma$, given by\n\n$$\nμ = \\begin{bmatrix}μ_{1} \\\\ μ_{2}\\end{bmatrix}, Σ = \\begin{bmatrix}σ_{1}^{2} & σ_{12} \\\\ σ_{21} & σ_{2}^{2}\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nthen the random variable $Y$, defined as\n\n$$\nY= aX_1 + bX_2\n$$\n\n