r{blue}\\sigma^2})^{-1/2} \\: \\mathrm{exp}\\left(-\\frac{1}{2} (x-\\mu) ({\\color{blue}\\sigma^2})^{-1} (x-\\mu) \\right)\n$$\n\nLet's compare this to the density function of a bivariate normal vector. First, we rewrite the joint density $f(x_1,x_2)$ of the bivariate normal distribution as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf(x_{1}, x_{2}) &= \\frac{1}{2π\\sqrt{\\mid Σ \\mid}}\\exp(-\\frac{1}{2}(X - μ)^{T}Σ^{-1}(X - μ)) \\\\\n&= \\left.(2π)^{-1} \\mid Σ\\right|^{-1/2}\\exp(-\\frac{1}{2}(X - μ)^{T}Σ^{-1}(X - μ)) \\\\\n&= \\left.(2π)^{-2/2} \\mid Σ\\right|^{-1/2}\\exp(-\\frac{1}{2}(X - μ)^{T}Σ^{-1}(X - μ))\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBy comparing the expressions for $f(x)$ and $f(x_1,x_2)$, we see that the *bivariate* normal distribution can be considered as a generalization of the *normal distribution* from the ${\\color{red}1}$-dimensional case (when we have only one variable, $x$) to the ${\\color{red}2}$-dimensional case (when we have two variables, $x_1$ and $x_2$).\n\nThis analogy will be particularly helpful when extending the bivariate normal distribution to more than two random variables." 13194139535959 17592186045417 false] [17592186093484 1064 "A linear combination of coordinates of a bivariate normal random vector generates a (one-dimensional) normal distribution:\n\n*If $\\mathbf{X} = [X_1, X_2]^T$ is a bivariate normal random vector with mean vector $\\boldsymbol{\\mu}$ and covariance matrix $\\Sigma,$ given by*\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mu =[\\begin{aligned}{\\mu }_{1} \\\\ {\\mu }_{2}\\end{aligned}],\\,Σ=[\\begin{aligned}{\\sigma }_{1}^{2} & {\\sigma }_{12} \\\\ {\\sigma }_{21} & {\\sigma }_{2}^{2}\\end{aligned}],\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n*then the random variable $Y,$ defined as *\n\n$$\nY= aX_1 + bX_2\n$$\n\n*is a normally distributed random variable for all values of $a$ and $b.$ Furthermore, we have that*\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}E[Y] & =a{\\mu }_{1}+b{\\mu }_{2}, \\\\ Var[Y] & ={a}^{2}{\\sigma }_{1}^{2}+{b}^{2}{\\sigma }_{2}^{2}+2ab{\\sigma }_{12}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535959 17592186045417 true] [17592186093484 1064 "A linear combination of coordinates of a bivariate normal random vector generates a (one-dimensional) normal distribution:\n\n> *If $\\mathbf{X} = [X_1, X_2]^T$ is a bivariate normal random vector with mean vector $\\boldsymbol{\\mu}$ and covariance matrix $\\Sigma$, given by*\n> $μ = \\begin{bmatrix}μ_{1} \\\\ μ_{2}\\end{bmatrix}, Σ = \\begin{bmatrix}σ_{1}^{2} & σ_{12} \\\\ σ_{21} & σ_{2}^{2}\\end{bmatrix}$,\n> *then the random variable $Y$, defined as*\n> $Y= aX_1 + bX_2$\n> *is a normally distributed random variable for all values of $a$ and $b$. Furthermore, we have that*\n> $E[Y]|= aμ_{1} + bμ_{2},; Var[Y]|= a^{2}σ_{1}^{2} + b^{2}σ_{2}^{2} + 2abσ_{12}$." 13194139535959 17592186045417 false] [17592186093485 1064 "Let's consider a bivariate normal vector\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{X} = [X_1, X_2]^T\n$$\n\nwith the joint probability density function\n\n$$\nf(x_1, x_2) = \\dfrac{1}{2\\pi\\sqrt{\\mid \\Sigma \\mid}} \\mathrm{exp}\\left(-\\dfrac 12(\\mathbf X - \\boldsymbol \\mu)^T \\,\\Sigma^{-1} \\,(\\mathbf X - \\boldsymbol \\mu)\\right)\n$$\n\nwhere the mean vector $\\boldsymbol{\\mu}$ and covariance matrix $\\Sigma$ are given by\n\n$$\nμ = \\begin{bmatrix}μ_{1} \\\\ μ_{2}\\end{bmatrix}, Σ = \\begin{bmatrix}σ_{1}^{2} & σ_{12} \\\\ σ_{21} & σ_{2}^{2}\\end{bmatrix} = \\begin{bmatrix}σ_{1}^{2} & ρσ_{1}σ_{2} \\\\ ρσ_{1}σ_{2} & σ_{2}^{2}\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nMost of the parameters of the distribution can be interpreted geometrically on a corresponding graph of the probability density function:\n\n- The mean vector $\\boldsymbol{\\mu}$ gives the $xy$-coordinates of the peak.\n- The correlation coefficient $\\rho$ defines how much the bell is squeezed towards the plane $y=x{:}$ when $\\rho \\to 0$, the horizontal cross-sections are almost circular, whilewhen $\\rho \\to 1$, the shape becomes more and more squeezed towards the plane $y=x$.\n\n![](images/5b/5bd69db417134cccedd5af45cb56fb44f27925ba5a8470f3b5622f0856ed855d.png)\n\n- The variances $\\sigma_1^2$ and $\\sigma_2^2$ represent how much the curve is spread out along the principal axes $x_1=\\mu_1$ and $x_2=\\mu_2{:}$when $\\sigma_1^2$ and $\\sigma_2^2$ are small, the bell shape is more \"pointy,\" whilefor larger $\\sigma_1^2$ and $\\sigma_2^2$, the bell shape is more \"flat.\"\n\nThe level curves of the surface have equations of the form\n\n$$\n(\\mathbf X - \\boldsymbol \\mu)^T \\,\\Sigma^{-1} \\,(\\mathbf X - \\boldsymbol \\mu) = \\textrm{const}\n$$\n\nwhich represent ellipses centered at $(\\mu_1,\\mu_2)$.\n\n![](images/1f/1f6b5643b7ab0568faa0b6e16b55a11482000b09d1b5862158d4ac9649281bb7.png)" 13194139535959 17592186045417 false] [17592186093485 1064 "Let's consider a bivariate normal vector $\\mathbf{X} = [X_1, X_2]^T$ with the joint probability density function \n\n$$\nf(x_1, x_2) = \\dfrac{1}{2\\pi\\sqrt{|\\Sigma|}} \\mathrm{exp}\\left( -\\dfrac 12(\\mathbf X - \\boldsymbol \\mu)^T \\,\\Sigma^{-1} \\,(\\mathbf X - \\boldsymbol \\mu)\\right),\n$$\n\nwhere the mean vector $\\boldsymbol{\\mu}$ and covariance matrix $\\Sigma$ are given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mu =[\\begin{aligned}{\\mu }_{1} \\\\ {\\mu }_{2}\\end{aligned}],\\,Σ=[\\begin{aligned}{\\sigma }_{1}^{2} & {\\sigma }_{12} \\\\ {\\sigma }_{21} & {\\sigma }_{2}^{2}\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}{\\sigma }_{1}^{2} & \\rho {\\sigma }_{1}{\\sigma }_{2} \\\\ \\rho {\\sigma }_{1}{\\sigma }_{2} & {\\sigma }_{2}^{2}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nMost of the parameters of the distribution can be interpreted geometrically on a corresponding graph of the probability density function:\n\n- The mean vector $\\boldsymbol{\\mu}$ gives the $xy$-coordinates of the peak.\n\n- The correlation coefficient $\\rho$ defines how much the bell is squeezed towards the plane $y=x{:}$ \n\n- when $\\rho \\to 0,$ the horizontal cross-sections are almost circular, while\n\n- when $\\rho \\to 1,$ the shape becomes more and more squeezed towards the plane $y=x.$\n\n![](images/5b/5bd69db417134cccedd5af45cb56fb44f27925ba5a8470f3b5622f0856ed855d.png)\n\n \n\n- The variances $\\sigma_1^2$ and $\\sigma_2^2$ represent how much the curve is spread out along the principal axes $x_1=\\mu_1$ and $x_2=\\mu_2{:}$\n\n- when $\\sigma_1^2$ and $\\sigma_2^2$ are small, the bell shape is more \"pointy,\" while\n\n- for larger $\\sigma_1^2$ and $\\sigma_2^2,$ the bell shape is more \"flat.\"\n\nThe level curves of the surface have equations of the form\n\n$$\n(\\mathbf X - \\boldsymbol \\mu)^T \\,\\Sigma^{-1} \\,(\\mathbf X - \\boldsymbol \\mu) = \\textrm{const},\n$$\n\n \n\nwhich represent ellipses centered at $(\\mu_1,\\mu_2).$\n\n![](images/1f/1f6b5643b7ab0568faa0b6e16b55a11482000b09d1b5862158d4ac9649281bb7.png)" 13194139535959 17592186045417 true] [17592186100029 1075 17592186100030 13194139535959 17592186045417 false] [17592186100029 1075 17592186100031 13194139535959 17592186045417 false] [17592186100029 1075 17592186100032 13194139535959 17592186045417 false] [17592186100029 1075 17592186100033 13194139535959 17592186045417 false] [17592186100029 1075 17592186100034 13194139535959 17592186045417 false] [17592186100029 1075 17592186548397 13194139535959 17592186045417 true] [17592186100029 1075 17592186548535 13194139535959 17592186045417 true] [17592186100029 1075 17592186548602 13194139535959 17592186045417 true] [17592186100029 1075 17592186548728 13194139535959 17592186045417 true] [17592186100029 1075 17592186548780 13194139535959 17592186045417 true] [17592186100029 1078 17592186548780 13194139535959 17592186045417 true] [17592186100845 1038 17592186549040 13194139535959 17592186045417 true] [17592186100845 1038 17592186549057 13194139535959 17592186045417 true] [17592186100944 1038 17592186549010 13194139535959 17592186045417 true] [17592186100944 1038 17592186549019 13194139535959 17592186045417 true] [17592186101478 1070 17592186045487 13194139535959 17592186045417 true] [17592186101478 1071 "![](images/eb/ebe962cc3394e0839d77fba922a5a7f56f31201f6926ef8b4236c1f82edd2e90.png)\n\nConsider a right triangle inscribed in a semicircle of radius\n$5,$ as shown above. What is the greatest possible perimeter of the triangle?" 13194139535959 17592186045417 true] [17592186101478 1071 "![](images/ed/ed3c3c06ea15b6f5950dbe73a011fd20f4ad27f64c66a3e5527e396c12e7078a.png)\n\nConsider a right triangle inscribed in a semi-circle of radius\n$5$, as shown above. What is the greatest possible perimeter of the triangle?\n\n> A scientific calculator is required to answer this question." 13194139535959 17592186045417 false] [17592186101478 1074 "Let's call $l$ the length of one of the legs. By the Pythagorean theorem, the length of the other leg is $\\sqrt{100-{l}^{2}}.$\n\nTherefore, the perimeter is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(l) & =10+l+\\sqrt{100-{l}^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $l$ is the length of a leg of a right triangle whose hypotenuse is $10,$ the permitted range of $l$ is $0<l<10.$\n\nWe want to maximize $P(l),$ so we need to compute the critical points. Taking the derivative, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}^{′}(l) & =1+\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot \\frac{-2l}{\\sqrt{100-{l}^{2}}} \\\\  & =1-\\frac{l}{\\sqrt{100-{l}^{2}}},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand solving ${P}^{′}(l)=0$ gives the following critical point:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}^{′}(l) & =0 \\\\ 1-\\frac{l}{\\sqrt{100-{l}^{2}}} & =0 \\\\ \\frac{l}{\\sqrt{100-{l}^{2}}} & =1 \\\\ l & =\\sqrt{100-{l}^{2}} \\\\ {l}^{2} & =100-{l}^{2} \\\\ 2{l}^{2} & =100 \\\\ {l}^{2} & =50 \\\\ l & =5\\sqrt{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAll that's left now is to confirm that $l=5\\sqrt{2}$ does indeed give a maximum value of $P(l).$ To do this, we use the first derivative test. We write up a table of values:\n\n| Interval | $(0,5\\sqrt{2})$ | $5\\sqrt{2}$ | $(5\\sqrt{2},10)$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Test Point | $1$ | $$ | $8$ |\n| ${P}^{′}$ | $+$ | $0$ | $-$ |\n| $P$ | $↗$ | $\\text{max}$ | $↘$ |\n\nSo $l=5\\sqrt{2}$ does indeed give a maximum, and the perimeter of the resulting triangle will be the largest possible.\n\nTherefore, the greatest perimeter that a triangle can have is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(5\\sqrt{2}) & =10+5\\sqrt{2}+\\sqrt{100-(5\\sqrt{2})^{2}} \\\\  & =10+5\\sqrt{2}+\\sqrt{100-50} \\\\  & =10+5\\sqrt{2}+\\sqrt{50} \\\\  & =10+5\\sqrt{2}+5\\sqrt{2} \\\\  & =10+10\\sqrt{2} \\\\  & =10(1+\\sqrt{2}).\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535959 17592186045417 true] [17592186104050 1075 17592186104051 13194139535959 17592186045417 false] [17592186104050 1075 17592186104052 13194139535959 17592186045417 false] [17592186104050 1075 17592186104053 13194139535959 17592186045417 false] [17592186104050 1075 17592186104054 13194139535959 17592186045417 false] [17592186104050 1075 17592186104055 13194139535959 17592186045417 false] [17592186104050 1075 17592186548386 13194139535959 17592186045417 true] [17592186104050 1075 17592186548516 13194139535959 17592186045417 true] [17592186104050 1075 17592186548680 13194139535959 17592186045417 true] [17592186104050 1075 17592186548708 13194139535959 17592186045417 true] [17592186104050 1075 17592186548711 13194139535959 17592186045417 true] [17592186104050 1078 17592186548516 13194139535959 17592186045417 true] [17592186105718 1038 17592186549028 13194139535959 17592186045417 true] [17592186105719 1038 17592186549030 13194139535959 17592186045417 true] [17592186105871 1038 17592186549052 13194139535959 17592186045417 true] [17592186105872 1038 17592186549027 13194139535959 17592186045417 true] [17592186105924 1038 17592186549022 13194139535959 17592186045417 true] [17592186105924 1038 17592186549058 13194139535959 17592186045417 true] [17592186106317 1070 17592186045487 13194139535959 17592186045417 true] [17592186106317 1071 "![](images/48/48d9d8513f45b87f9deffeffcef31e49a6dcaba0b033c91a918c1d72d87eb215.png)\n\nIn the quadrilateral $ABCD$ above, determine the measure of $∠C$." 13194139535959 17592186045417 false] [17592186106317 1071 "![](images/48/48d9d8513f45b87f9deffeffcef31e49a6dcaba0b033c91a918c1d72d87eb215.png)\n\nIn the quadrilateral $ABCD$ above, determine the measure of $∠C.$" 13194139535959 17592186045417 true] [17592186106317 1074 "A quadrilateral has $n=4$ sides. Therefore, the sum of the interior angles of a quadrilateral  must be \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{180}^{∘}\\cdot (n-2) & ={180}^{∘}\\cdot (4-2) \\\\  & ={180}^{∘}\\cdot 2 \\\\  & ={360}^{∘}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAdding up the internal angles and solving for $x,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m∠A+m∠B+m∠C+m∠D & ={360}^{∘} \\\\ x+{90}^{∘}+3x+6x & ={360}^{∘} \\\\ 10x+{90}^{∘} & ={360}^{∘} \\\\ 10x & ={270}^{∘} \\\\ x & ={27}^{∘}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m∠C & =3x \\\\  & =3({27}^{∘}) \\\\  & ={81}^{∘}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535959 17592186045417 true] [17592186110720 1038 17592186549009 13194139535959 17592186045417 true] [17592186110720 1038 17592186549054 13194139535959 17592186045417 true] [17592186117192 1075 17592186548363 13194139535959 17592186045417 true] [17592186117192 1078 17592186548363 13194139535959 17592186045417 true] [17592186119950 1038 17592186548992 13194139535959 17592186045417 true] [17592186119950 1038 17592186549032 13194139535959 17592186045417 true] [17592186119951 1038 17592186548977 13194139535959 17592186045417 true] [17592186119951 1038 17592186548998 13194139535959 17592186045417 true] [17592186119951 1038 17592186549041 13194139535959 17592186045417 true] [17592186119952 1038 17592186549059 13194139535959 17592186045417 true] [17592186119952 1038 17592186549061 13194139535959 17592186045417 true] [17592186119953 1038 17592186548984 13194139535959 17592186045417 true] [17592186119953 1038 17592186549046 13194139535959 17592186045417 true] [17592186120171 1070 17592186045487 13194139535959 17592186045417 true] [17592186120171 1071 "Consider the following geometric sequence:\n\n$$\n{a}_{n+1}=\\frac{1}{4}{a}_{n},\\,{a}_{1}=256,\\,n\\ge 1\n$$\n\nThe $4$th term of this geometric sequence is ${a}_{4}=$\n{{field-1}}." 13194139535959 17592186045417 true] [17592186120171 1071 "Consider the following geometric sequence:\n$a_{n + 1} = \\frac{1}{4}a_{n}, a_{1} = 256, n \\ge 1$The $4$th term of this geometric sequence is $a_{4} =$\n{{field-1}}." 13194139535959 17592186045417 false] [17592186120171 1074 "We use the recursive formula to compute the terms of the sequence up to the $4$th term:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{1} & =256 \\\\ {a}_{2} & =\\frac{1}{4}{a}_{1}=\\frac{1}{4}(256)=64 \\\\ {a}_{3} & =\\frac{1}{4}{a}_{2}=\\frac{1}{4}(64)=16 \\\\ {a}_{4} & =\\frac{1}{4}{a}_{3}=\\frac{1}{4}(16)=4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, ${a}_{4}=4.$" 13194139535959 17592186045417 true] [17592186124509 1038 17592186549053 13194139535959 17592186045417 true] [17592186124525 1038 17592186549018 13194139535959 17592186045417 true] [17592186133925 1038 17592186549016 13194139535959 17592186045417 true] [17592186134074 1074 "Let $C$ be a positively oriented, piecewise-smooth, simple closed curve in the plane, and let $D$ be the region bounded by $C.$ If $P$ and $Q$ have continuous first partial derivatives on an open region containing $D$, then Green's theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}P\\,dx+Q\\,dy={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nThe region $D$ and the curve $C$ for this case are shown below.\n\n![](images/7c/7ca4b6fe3a5a16c4004c75b0e4524cc3b42550cb2413d5b8e1c21a1d53cee902.png)\n\nWe have that $P(x,y)={e}^{x}-2{x}^{2}y$ and $Q(x,y)={e}^{y}+2x{y}^{2}.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=-2{x}^{2},\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂x} & =2{y}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can express the region $D$ as a type II region as follows:\n\n$$\nD=\\{(x,y):-y\\le x\\le y,\\,0\\le y\\le 2\\}\n$$\n\nTherefore, applying Green's Theorem, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{C}({e}^{x}-2{x}^{2}y)\\,dx+({e}^{y}+2x{y}^{2})\\,dy & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(2{y}^{2}+2{x}^{2})\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{-y}^{y}(2{x}^{2}+2{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}2{[\\frac{{x}^{3}}{3}+x{y}^{2}]}_{-y}^{y}\\,dy \\\\  & =2{\\int }_{0}^{2}(\\frac{{y}^{3}}{3}+{y}^{3}+\\frac{{y}^{3}}{3}+{y}^{3})dy \\\\  & =2{\\int }_{0}^{2}\\frac{8{y}^{3}}{3}\\,dy \\\\  & =\\frac{16}{3}{\\int }_{0}^{2}{y}^{3}\\,dy \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{3}{[{y}^{4}]}_{0}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{3}\\cdot {2}^{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{64}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535959 17592186045417 true] [17592186134074 1074 "Let $C$ be a positively oriented, piecewise-smooth, simple closed curve in the plane, and let $D$ be the region bounded by $C.$ If $P$ and $Q$ have continuous first partial derivatives on an open region containing $D$, then Green's theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}P\\,dx+Q\\,dy={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nThe region $D$ and the curve $C$ for this case are shown below.\n\n![](images/7c/7ca4b6fe3a5a16c4004c75b0e4524cc3b42550cb2413d5b8e1c21a1d53cee902.png)\n\nWe have that $P(x,y)={e}^{x}-2{x}^{2}y$ and $Q(x,y)={e}^{y}+2x{y}^{2}.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=-2{x}^{2},\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂x} & =2{y}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can express the region $D$ as a type II region as follows:\n\n$$\nD={(x,y):-y\\le x\\le y,\\,0\\le y\\le 2}\n$$\n\nTherefore, applying Green's Theorem, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{C}({e}^{x}-2{x}^{2}y)\\,dx+({e}^{y}+2x{y}^{2})\\,dy & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(2{y}^{2}+2{x}^{2})\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{-y}^{y}(2{x}^{2}+2{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}2{[\\frac{{x}^{3}}{3}+x{y}^{2}]}_{-y}^{y}\\,dy \\\\  & =2{\\int }_{0}^{2}(\\frac{{y}^{3}}{3}+{y}^{3}+\\frac{{y}^{3}}{3}+{y}^{3})dy \\\\  & =2{\\int }_{0}^{2}\\frac{8{y}^{3}}{3}\\,dy \\\\  & =\\frac{16}{3}{\\int }_{0}^{2}{y}^{3}\\,dy \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{3}{[{y}^{4}]}_{0}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{3}\\cdot {2}^{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{64}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535959 17592186045417 false] [17592186134127 1074 "![](images/c1/c1853c772e219f53a0b394bd0b50beea6b33c451afc453983cdccb1df333c5ea.png)\n\n \n\nNotice that our solid can be written as the type III region\n\n$$\nR=\\{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,(x,z)\\in D,\\,\\,0\\le y\\le 4-x-z\\},\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n$$\nD=\\{(x,z)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le 1,\\,\\,0\\le z\\le 2\\}\n$$\n\nis the projection of $R$ onto the $xz$-plane, shown below.\n\n![](images/06/0620f8f25174cb71528049341669458c68a7ccfdd3ff76b5f62c878307080419.png)\n\nHence, the type III representation of $R$ is\n\n$$\nR=\\{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le 1,\\,\\,0\\le z\\le 2,\\,\\,0\\le y\\le 4-x-z\\}\n$$\n\nAs a result, by writing down our triple integral as a repeated integral, we obtain\n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}12xz\\,\\text{d}V={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{4-x-z}12xz\\,dy\\,dz\\,dx.\n$$\n\nFirst, we evaluate the inner integral with respect to $y$, treating $x$ and $z$ as constants: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{4-x-z}12xz\\,dy\\,dz\\,dx & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{2}[{\\int }_{0}^{4-x-z}12xz\\,dy]dz\\,dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{2}12xz[y]_{y=0}^{y=4-x-z}\\,dz\\,dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{2}12xz(4-x-z)\\,dz\\,dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{2}(48xz-12{x}^{2}z-12x{z}^{2})\\,dz\\,dx\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we evaluate the inner integral with respect to $z$, treating $x$ as a constant: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{2}(48xz-12{x}^{2}z-12x{z}^{2})\\,dz\\,dx & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}[{\\int }_{0}^{2}(48xz-12{x}^{2}z-12x{z}^{2})\\,dz]dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}[24x{z}^{2}-6{x}^{2}{z}^{2}-4x{z}^{3}]_{z=0}^{z=2}\\,dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(64x-24{x}^{2})\\,dx\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we integrate with respect to $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{1}(64x-24{x}^{2})\\,dx & ={[32{x}^{2}-8{x}^{3}]}_{x=0}^{x=1} \\\\  & =(32-8)-0 \\\\  & =24.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535959 17592186045417 true] [17592186134127 1074 "![](images/c1/c1853c772e219f53a0b394bd0b50beea6b33c451afc453983cdccb1df333c5ea.png)\n\n \n\nNotice 