92186045417 true] [17592186152594 1070 17592186045487 13194139535959 17592186045417 true] [17592186152594 1071 "![](images/c4/c46e88b9e044fccb91e0efa4ee981973a286e0335f58a70f7b1f9a856d9bfed4.png)\n\nFor which of the following values of $x$ does the function $y = f(x)$, shown above, attain a local minimum?\n\n1. $x = 3$\n2. $x = 2$\n3. $x =-2$" 13194139535959 17592186045417 false] [17592186152594 1071 "![](images/c4/c46e88b9e044fccb91e0efa4ee981973a286e0335f58a70f7b1f9a856d9bfed4.png)\n\nFor which of the following values of $x$ does the function $y=f(x),$ shown above, attain a local minimum?\n\nI. $x=3$\n\nII. $x=2$\n\nIII. $x=-2$" 13194139535959 17592186045417 true] [17592186152594 1074 "Let's examine each of the given points in turn.\n\n- The function has a local minimum at $x=3.$ For example, in the interval $(2.75,3.25),$ the smallest value of the function is attained at $x=3,$ as shown below. \n\n![](images/61/6115a87572fb93b9e3dcf69c75cd4a5efaae2dbbea90b28cb57cdd05b8db11b7.png)\n\nWe can write this mathematically as $f(x)\\ge f(3)\\,$ for all $\\,x\\in (2.75,3.25).$\n\n- The function does *not* have a local minimum at $x=2.$ For any open interval containing $x=2$, there will be points on the graph that lie below $f(2).$\n\n- The function has a local minimum at $x=-2.$ For example, in the interval $(-2.5,-1.5),$ the smallest value of the function is attained at $x=-2,$ as shown below. \n\n![](images/26/26acee415fa4a1038077d11f88b7c61c8b45f4aa857cc578af192e4085f7c005.png)\n\nWe can write this mathematically as $f(x)\\ge f(2)\\,$ for all $\\,x\\in (-2.5,-1.5).$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\"" 13194139535959 17592186045417 true] [17592186153362 1064 "A **local extremum** (or **relative extremum**) of a function is a point that is either a maximum or minimum point of the function relative to an (open) interval in the function's domain.\n\nFor example, consider the function shown in the graph below.\n\n![](images/19/197834de138cb2e05a3af391bbeec1279ad8bfddfb277b881e5e3eeb49bcb73a.png)\n\nThe graph has a peak at the point $A$ where $x=0$. We say that the function has a **local maximum** when $x=0$. We also say that the point $A$ is a **local maximum point** of the function.\n\nThis means that we can take an open interval of $x$-values around $x=0$ such that $A$ will be the highest point on the graph in this interval.\n\n![](images/d5/d522b4b0a59ff328399493bc12a770af2d99a1587b2826accb9835d7463ef9ba.png)\n\nSimilarly, the graph has a valley at the point $B$ where $x=2$. We say that the function has a **local minimum** when $x=2$. We can also say that the point $B$ is a **local minimum point** of the function.\n\nThis means that we can take an open interval of $x$-values around $x=2$ such that $B$ will be the lowest point on the graph in this interval.\n\n![](images/e2/e2a92d4217e1eebcc724e48cca273d2e707731ded914207e59fff149b46b4dcc.png)" 13194139535959 17592186045417 false] [17592186153362 1064 "A **local extremum** (or **relative extremum**) of a function is a point that is either a maximum or minimum point of the function relative to an (open) interval in the function's domain. \n\nFor example, consider the function shown in the graph below. \n\n![](images/19/197834de138cb2e05a3af391bbeec1279ad8bfddfb277b881e5e3eeb49bcb73a.png)\n\nThe graph has a peak at the point $A$ where $x=0.$ We say that the function has a **local maximum** when $x=0.$ We also say that the point $A$ is a **local maximum point** of the function. \n\nThis means that we can take an open interval of $x$-values around $x=0$ such that $A$ will be the highest point on the graph in this interval. \n\n![](images/d5/d522b4b0a59ff328399493bc12a770af2d99a1587b2826accb9835d7463ef9ba.png)\n\nSimilarly, the graph has a valley at the point $B$ where $x=2.$ We say that the function has a **local minimum** when $x=2.$ We can also say that the point $B$ is a **local minimum point** of the function.\n\nThis means that we can take an open interval of $x$-values around $x=2$ such that $B$ will be the lowest point on the graph in this interval. \n\n![](images/e2/e2a92d4217e1eebcc724e48cca273d2e707731ded914207e59fff149b46b4dcc.png)" 13194139535959 17592186045417 true] [17592186153406 1064 "Can a point on a graph be both a local minimum *and* a local maximum?\n\nTo answer this question, let's consider the function $f(x)$ given by the graph below.\n\n![](images/0c/0cec91d9ec8e108ee4836e816aae63722fc4c09d997790eaf283cd3e2a9c4bc6.png)\n\nNow consider the point where $x=2$. Our goal is to determine which type of extremum it is. To do this, we consider the values of $f(x)$ in the open interval $(1.5, 2.5)$, shown below.\n\n![](images/c6/c6cf1a6237c50d5c2b651937ca46b9ae52a08dafb831d76b27ddb068d37dd501.png)\n\nNow, notice the following:\n\n- The point where $x=2$ is a local maximum because $f(x) \\le f(2) \\:$ for all $\\: x \\in (1.5,2.5)\\quad {\\color{\\green}\\checkmark}$\n- The point where $x=2$ is a local minimum because $f(x) \\ge f(2) \\:$ for all $\\: x \\in (1.5,2.5)\\quad {\\color{\\green}\\checkmark}$\n\nTherefore, $x=2$ is both a local maximum *and* a local minimum of $f(x)$!\n\nThis might seem strange. But, notice that $x=2$ lies within an interval where the function is constant. This is a special case where a particular point is both a local minimum and a local maximum." 13194139535959 17592186045417 false] [17592186153406 1064 "Can a point on a graph be both a local minimum *and* a local maximum?\n\nTo answer this question, let's consider the function $f(x)$ given by the graph below. \n\n![](images/0c/0cec91d9ec8e108ee4836e816aae63722fc4c09d997790eaf283cd3e2a9c4bc6.png)\n\nNow consider the point where $x=2.$ Our goal is to determine which type of extremum it is. To do this, we consider the values of $f(x)$ in the open interval $(1.5, 2.5),$ shown below.\n\n![](images/c6/c6cf1a6237c50d5c2b651937ca46b9ae52a08dafb831d76b27ddb068d37dd501.png)\n\nNow, notice the following:\n\n- The point where $x=2$ is a local maximum because $f(x) \\le f(2) \\:$ for all $\\: x \\in (1.5,2.5)\\quad {\\color{\\green}\\checkmark}$\n\n- The point where $x=2$ is a local minimum because $f(x) \\ge f(2) \\:$ for all $\\: x \\in (1.5,2.5)\\quad {\\color{\\green}\\checkmark}$\n\nTherefore, $x=2$ is both a local maximum *and* a local minimum of $f(x)!$ \n\nThis might seem strange. But, notice that $x=2$ lies within an interval where the function is constant. This is a special case where a particular point is both a local minimum and a local maximum." 13194139535959 17592186045417 true] [17592186153407 1064 "Let's go back to looking at the function $y=f(x)$ that we saw earlier.\n\n![](images/19/197834de138cb2e05a3af391bbeec1279ad8bfddfb277b881e5e3eeb49bcb73a.png)\n\nThe point $A$ where $x=0$ is a local maximum of $f(x)$ because we can draw an open interval around $x=0$ such that the point $A$ is the highest point on the graph. One such interval is $x\\in (-0.5,0.5)$, shown below:\n\n![](images/d5/d522b4b0a59ff328399493bc12a770af2d99a1587b2826accb9835d7463ef9ba.png)\n\nEverywhere in this interval, the value of the function is *less than or equal to* $f(0)$, the largest value of the function in this interval. We can write this mathematically as\n\n$\\qquad$\n\n$$\nf(x) \\leq f(0)\n$$\n\nfor all $x\\in(-0.5, 0.5)$.\n\nSimilarly, the point $B$ where $x=2$ is a local minimum of $f(x)$ because we can draw an open interval around $x=2$ such that the point $B$ is the lowest point on the graph. One such interval is $x\\in (1.5,2.5)$, shown below:\n\n![](images/e2/e2a92d4217e1eebcc724e48cca273d2e707731ded914207e59fff149b46b4dcc.png)\n\nEverywhere in this interval, the value of the function is *greater than or equal to* $f(2)$, the smallest value of the function in this interval. We can write this mathematically as\n\n$\\qquad$\n\n$$\nf(x) \\geq f(2)\n$$\n\nfor all $x\\in(1.5, 2.5)$." 13194139535959 17592186045417 false] [17592186153407 1064 "Let's go back to looking at the function $y=f(x)$ that we saw earlier.\n\n![](images/19/197834de138cb2e05a3af391bbeec1279ad8bfddfb277b881e5e3eeb49bcb73a.png)\n\nThe point $A$ where $x=0$ is a local maximum of $f(x)$ because we can draw an open interval around $x=0$ such that the point $A$ is the highest point on the graph. One such interval is $x\\in (-0.5,0.5),$ shown below:\n\n![](images/d5/d522b4b0a59ff328399493bc12a770af2d99a1587b2826accb9835d7463ef9ba.png)\n\nEverywhere in this interval, the value of the function is *less than or equal to* $f(0),$ the largest value of the function in this interval. We can write this mathematically as\n\n$\\qquad$ $f(x) \\leq f(0)$ for all $x\\in(-0.5, 0.5).$\n\nSimilarly, the point $B$ where $x=2$ is a local minimum of $f(x)$ because we can draw an open interval around $x=2$ such that the point $B$ is the lowest point on the graph. One such interval is $x\\in (1.5,2.5),$ shown below:\n\n![](images/e2/e2a92d4217e1eebcc724e48cca273d2e707731ded914207e59fff149b46b4dcc.png)\n\nEverywhere in this interval, the value of the function is *greater than or equal to* $f(2),$ the smallest value of the function in this interval. We can write this mathematically as\n\n$\\qquad$ $f(x) \\geq f(2)$ for all $x\\in(1.5, 2.5).$" 13194139535959 17592186045417 true] [17592186155258 1075 17592186548859 13194139535959 17592186045417 true] [17592186155258 1078 17592186548859 13194139535959 17592186045417 true] [17592186155259 1075 17592186548844 13194139535959 17592186045417 true] [17592186155259 1078 17592186548844 13194139535959 17592186045417 true] [17592186156297 1075 17592186156298 13194139535959 17592186045417 false] [17592186156297 1075 17592186156299 13194139535959 17592186045417 false] [17592186156297 1075 17592186156300 13194139535959 17592186045417 false] [17592186156297 1075 17592186156301 13194139535959 17592186045417 false] [17592186156297 1075 17592186156302 13194139535959 17592186045417 false] [17592186156297 1075 17592186548371 13194139535959 17592186045417 true] [17592186156297 1075 17592186548597 131941