52.png)\n\nThe phase portrait for a system of linear differential equations whose matrix has two complex eigenvalues $\\lambda =a\\pm b\\text{i},$ where $a,b\\in R,$ is shown above. Select the correct options in the reasoning below.\n\nThe given curve is \n{{field-1}},\nrepresenting\n{{field-2}}\nthat is traversed \n{{field-3}}.\nThus, we must have \n{{field-4}}." 13194139535959 17592186045417 true] [17592186167229 1071 "![](images/9a/9aa8e2fec9812145eba6ef3add63dfcf16c9ddeb5557c3d37ef511947ace2e52.png)\n\nThe phase portrait for a system of linear differential equations whose matrix has two complex eigenvalues $λ = a ± b\\textrm{i}$, where $a, b ∈ R$, is shown above. Select the correct options in the reasoning below.The given curve is\n{{field-1}},\nrepresenting\n{{field-2}}\nthat is traversed\n{{field-3}}.\nThus, we must have\n{{field-4}}." 13194139535959 17592186045417 false] [17592186167229 1074 "Let $v=p+q\\text{i},$ where $p,q\\in {R}^{2},$ be an eigenvector corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =a+b\\text{i}.$\n\nThen, a complex solution of the system is given by\n\n$$\n{x}_{\\text{complex}}(t)=v{e}^{\\lambda t}.\n$$\n\nUsing Euler's formula, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{\\text{complex}}(t) & =v{e}^{(a+b\\text{i})t} \\\\  & ={e}^{at}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(bt)+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(bt))(p+q\\text{i}) \\\\  & ={e}^{at}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(bt)p-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(bt)q)+\\text{i}{e}^{at}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(bt)p+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(bt)q).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general solution of the system is\n\n$$\n\\text{x}(t)={c}_{1}{e}^{at}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(bt)p-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(bt)q)+{c}_{2}{e}^{at}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(bt)p+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(bt)q),\n$$\n\nwhere the parts $(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(bt)p-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(bt)q)$ and $(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(bt)p+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(bt)q)$ are bounded.\n\nNow, notice that the given curve is $\\text{bounded}$ (and closed), representing $\\text{an ellipse}$ (circle) that is traversed $\\text{clockwise}.$\n\nBut we have that:\n\n- ${e}^{at}\\to \\infty $ as $t\\to \\infty $ when $a>0,$ and\n\n- ${e}^{at}\\to 0$ as $t\\to \\infty $ when $a<0.$\n\nThus, we must have $a=0.$" 13194139535959 17592186045417 true] [17592186175691 1071 "Compute the values of $\\theta \\in [0,\\pi]$ for which the polar curve $r=\\sin\\theta+\\cos\\theta$ has vertical tangents, given that\n\n$$\nx'(\\theta) = \\cos(2\\theta)-\\sin(2\\theta), \\qquad\ny'(\\theta) = \\cos(2\\theta)+\\sin(2\\theta).\n$$" 13194139535959 17592186045417 true] [17592186175691 1071 "Compute the values of $\\theta \\in [0,\\pi]$ for which the polar curve $r=\\sin\\theta+\\cos\\theta$ has vertical tangents, given that\n$x'(\\theta) = \\cos(2\\theta)-\\sin(2\\theta), \\qquad y'(\\theta) = \\cos(2\\theta)+\\sin(2\\theta)$." 13194139535959 17592186045417 false] [17592186175691 1074 "We have a vertical tangent when\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d x} = \\dfrac{y'(\\theta)}{x'(\\theta)}\n$$\n\nis infinite. This means that\n\n$$\nx'(\\theta) = 0 \\qquad \\textrm{and}\\qquad y'(\\theta) \\neq 0\n$$\n\nTo find the vertical tangents, we proceed as follows:\n\n**Step 1**: Compute the derivatives $y'(\\theta)$ and $x'(\\theta)$.\n\nWe're given that\n\n$$\nx'(\\theta) = \\cos(2\\theta)-\\sin(2\\theta), \\qquad y'(\\theta) = \\cos(2\\theta)+\\sin(2\\theta)\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Find the values of $\\theta$ where\n\n$$\nx'(\\theta)=0\n$$\n\nBy writing down and solving the equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\cos (2θ) - \\sin (2θ) &= 0 \\\\\n\\sin (2θ) &= \\cos (2θ) \\\\\n\\tan (2θ) &= 1 \\\\\n2θ &= \\frac{π}{4} + nπ \\\\\nθ &= \\frac{π}{8} + \\frac{nπ}{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ is any integer. However, among the solutions above, only two belong to the required domain, namely\n\n$$\n\\theta_1 = \\dfrac{\\pi}{8},\\qquad \\theta_2 = \\dfrac{5\\pi}{8}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Check to see whether any candidates have\n\n$$\ny'(\\theta) = 0\n$$\n\nWe substitute our candidate solutions into $y'(\\theta)$ and check that\n\n$$\ny'(\\theta) \\neq 0\n$$\n\nIf\n\n$$\ny'(\\theta) = 0\n$$\n\nfurther investigation is necessary.\n\n- Substituting $\\theta_1 =\\dfrac{\\pi}{8}$, we get\n$y^{′}(\\frac{π}{8})|= \\cos (\\frac{π}{4}) + \\sin (\\frac{π}{4}); = \\sqrt{2}; \\ne 0.✓$\nTherefore, $\\theta_1 =\\dfrac{\\pi}{8}$ is a valid solution.\n- Substituting $\\theta_2 =\\dfrac{5\\pi}{8}$, we get\n$y^{′}(\\frac{5π}{8})|= \\cos (\\frac{5π}{4}) + \\sin (\\frac{5π}{4}); =-\\sqrt{2}; \\ne 0.✓$\nTherefore, $\\theta_2 = \\dfrac{5\\pi}{8}$ is also a valid solution.\n\nTherefore, our curve has vertical tangents at\n\n$$\n\\theta = \\dfrac{\\pi}{8}, \\dfrac{5\\pi}{8}\n$$\n\n![](images/53/53562c50591938dcedf979a1dd4f0c1a9f0e43cade71e36f3f908bb0595a8794.png)" 13194139535959 17592186045417 false] [17592186175691 1074 "We have a vertical tangent when\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d x} = \\dfrac{y'(\\theta)}{x'(\\theta)}\n$$\n\nis infinite. This means that\n\n$$\nx'(\\theta) = 0 \\qquad \\textrm{and}\\qquad y'(\\theta) \\neq 0.\n$$\n\nTo find the vertical tangents, we proceed as follows:\n\n**Step 1**: Compute the derivatives $y'(\\theta)$ and $x'(\\theta).$ \n\nWe're given that\n\n$$\nx'(\\theta) = \\cos(2\\theta)-\\sin(2\\theta), \\qquad\ny'(\\theta) = \\cos(2\\theta)+\\sin(2\\theta).\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Find the values of $\\theta$ where $x'(\\theta)=0.$\n\nBy writing down and solving the equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\theta )-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta ) & =0 \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta ) & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\theta ) \\\\ \\operatorname{tan}⁡(2\\theta ) & =1 \\\\ 2\\theta  & =\\frac{\\pi }{4}+n\\pi  \\\\ \\theta  & =\\frac{\\pi }{8}+\\frac{n\\pi }{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ is any integer. However, among the solutions above, only two belong to the required domain, namely\n\n$$\n\\theta_1 = \\dfrac{\\pi}{8},\\qquad \\theta_2 = \\dfrac{5\\pi}{8} .\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Check to see whether any candidates have $y'(\\theta) = 0.$ \n\