theta}(\\cos\\theta+\\sin\\theta), \\qquad\ny'(\\theta)=e^{-\\theta}(-\\sin\\theta+\\cos\\theta).\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Find the values of $\\theta$ where $y'(\\theta)=0.$\n\nBy writing down and solving the equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{-\\theta }(-\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ) & =0 \\\\ -\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & =0 \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta  & =1 \\\\ \\theta  & =\\frac{\\pi }{4}+\\pi n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ is any integer. However, among the solutions above, only one belongs to the required domain, namely\n\n$$\n\\theta_1 = \\dfrac{\\pi}{4}.\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Check to see whether any candidates have $x'(\\theta) = 0.$ \n\nWe substitute our candidate solutions into $x'(\\theta)$ and check that $x'(\\theta) \\neq 0.$ If $x'(\\theta) = 0,$ further investigation is necessary.\n\n- Substituting $\\theta_1 = \\dfrac{\\pi}{4},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{4}) & =-{e}^{-\\pi /4}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4})+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4})) \\\\  & =-{e}^{-\\pi /4}(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}+\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}) \\\\  & =-\\sqrt{2}{e}^{-\\pi /4} \\\\  & \\ne 0.\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $\\theta_1 = \\dfrac{\\pi}{4}$ is a valid solution.\n\nTherefore, our curve has a horizontal tangent at $\\theta = \\dfrac{\\pi}{4}.$\n\n![](images/db/db7dea31d2eaeff23f4ebad30e2ea4deff098886a3a38b9d353af64307144939.png)" 13194139535959 17592186045417 true] [17592186175938 1038 17592186548980 13194139535959 17592186045417 true] [17592186175938 1038 17592186548988 13194139535959 17592186045417 true] [17592186175938 1038 17592186548996 13194139535959 17592186045417 true] [17592186175938 1038 17592186549036 13194139535959 17592186045417 true] [17592186175938 1038 17592186549043 13194139535959 17592186045417 true] [17592186176043 1038 17592186548986 13194139535959 17592186045417 true] [1759218