it seems that the curve\n\n$$\nr = 1+\\cos\\theta\n$$\n\nhas a horizontal tangent at\n\n$$\n\\theta = \\pi\n$$\n\nHowever, to be sure of this, we need to check that the tangent lines flatten out as we approach\n\n$$\n\\theta = \\pi\n$$\n\nIn other words, we need to check that\n\n$$\n\\lim\\limits_{\\theta \\to \\pi} \\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d x} = 0\n$$\n\nLet's now verify that this is true:\n\nUsing techniques discussed in previous lessons, it's straightforward to show that\n\n$$\nx'(\\theta)=-\\sin\\theta(2\\cos\\theta+1), \\qquad y'(\\theta)=2\\cos^2\\theta +\\cos\\theta - 1\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nlim_(θ → π)\\frac{dy}{dx} &= lim_(θ → π)((2\\cos^{2} θ + \\cos θ - 1)/(-\\sin θ(2\\cos θ + 1))) \\\\\n&=-lim_(θ → π)((2\\cos^{2} θ + \\cos θ - 1)/(\\sin θ(2\\cos θ + 1))) \\\\\n&=-lim_(θ → π)(\\frac{2\\cos^{2} θ + \\cos θ - 1}{2\\sin θ\\cos θ + \\sin θ}) \\\\\n&=-lim_(θ → π)(\\frac{2\\cos^{2} θ + \\cos θ - 1}{\\sin 2θ + \\sin θ})\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe already know that substituting\n\n$$\n\\theta = \\pi\n$$\n\ngives the indeterminate form\n\n$$\n\\dfrac00\n$$\n\nso evaluating this limit by direct substitution won't work. However, we can evaluate this limit using L'Hopital's rule.\n\nApplying L'Hopital's rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nlim_(θ → π)\\frac{dy}{dx} &= -lim_(θ → π)(\\frac{2\\cos^{2} θ + \\cos θ - 1}{\\sin 2θ + \\sin θ}) \\\\\n&=-lim_(θ → π)(((2\\cos^{2} θ + \\cos θ - 1)^{′})/((\\sin 2θ + \\sin θ)^{′})) \\\\\n&=-lim_(θ → π)(\\frac{-4\\cos θ\\sin θ - \\sin θ}{2\\cos 2θ + \\cos θ}) \\\\\n&= lim_(θ → π)(\\frac{4\\cos θ\\sin θ + \\sin θ}{2\\cos 2θ + \\cos θ}) \\\\\n&= \\frac{4\\cos π\\sin π + \\sin π}{2\\cos 2π + \\cos π} \\\\\n&= (4 \\cdot (-1) \\cdot (0) + 0)/(2 \\cdot 1 + (-1)) \\\\\n&= \\frac{0}{1} \\\\\n&= 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, since\n\n$$\n\\lim\\limits_{\\theta \\to \\pi} \\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d x} = 0\n$$\n\nwe conclude that our curve does indeed have a horizontal tangent at\n\n$$\n\\theta = \\pi\n$$\n\n![](images/f1/f172dbe1e7fa97fa924feed4ee193a620b1224a985a4e406ef4e46afbdc0cb2a.png)" 13194139535959 17592186045417 false] [17592186189391 1064 "So, it seems that the curve $r = 1+\\cos\\theta$ has a horizontal tangent at $\\theta = \\pi.$ However, to be sure of this, we need to check that the tangent lines flatten out as we approach $\\theta = \\pi.$ In other words, we need to check that\n\n \n\n$$\n\\lim\\limits_{\\theta \\to \\pi} \\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d x} = 0.\n$$\n\nLet's now verify that this is true:\n\nUsing techniques discussed in previous lessons, it's straightforward to show that\n\n$$\nx'(\\theta)=-\\sin\\theta(2\\cos\\theta+1), \\qquad \ny'(\\theta)=2\\cos^2\\theta +\\cos\\theta - 1.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{\\theta \\to \\pi }\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & ={lim}_{\\theta \\to \\pi }(\\frac{2{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -1}{-\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta (2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +1)}) \\\\  & =-{lim}_{\\theta \\to \\pi }(\\frac{2{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -1}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta (2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +1)}) \\\\  & =-{lim}_{\\theta \\to \\pi }(\\frac{2{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -1}{2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }) \\\\  & =-{lim}_{\\theta \\to \\pi }(\\frac{2{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -1}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\theta +\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe already know that substituting $\\theta = \\pi$ gives the indeterminate form $\\dfrac00,$ so evaluating this limit by direct substitution won't work. However, we can evaluate this limit using L'Hopital's rule.\n\nApplying L'Hopital's rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{\\theta \\to \\pi }\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =-{lim}_{\\theta \\to \\pi }(\\frac{2{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -1}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\theta +\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }) \\\\  & =-{lim}_{\\theta \\to \\pi }(\\frac{(2{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -1)^{′}}{(\\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\theta +\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )^{′}}) \\\\  & =-{lim}_{\\theta \\to \\pi }(\\frac{-4\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta -\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }{2\\operatorname{cos}⁡2\\theta +\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }) \\\\  & ={lim}_{\\theta \\to \\pi }(\\frac{4\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }{2\\operatorname{cos}⁡2\\theta +\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }) \\\\  & =\\frac{4\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\pi \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\pi +\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\pi }{2\\operatorname{cos}⁡2\\pi +\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\pi } \\\\  & =\\frac{4\\cdot (-1)\\cdot (0)+0}{2\\cdot 1+(-1)} \\\\  & =\\frac{0}{1} \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, since\n\n$$\n\\lim\\limits_{\\theta \\to \\pi} \\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d x} = 0\n$$\n\nwe conclude that our curve does indeed have a horizontal tangent at $\\theta = \\pi.$\n\n![](images/f1/f172dbe1e7fa97fa924feed4ee193a620b1224a985a4e406ef4e46afbdc0cb2a.png)" 13194139535959 17592186045417 true] [17592186220936 1075 17592186220937 13194139535959 17592186045417 false] [17592186220936 1075 17592186220938 13194139535959 17592186045417 false] [17592186220936 1075 17592186220939 13194139535959 17592186045417 false] [17592186220936 1075 17592186220940 13194139535959 17592186045417 false] [17592186220936 1075 17592186220941 13194139535959 17592186045417 false] [17592186220936 1075 17592186548505 13194139535959 17592186045417 true] [17592186220936 1075 17592186548525 13194139535959 17592186045417 true] [17592186220936 1075 17592186548607 13194139535959 17592186045417 true] [17592186220936 1075 17592186548661 13194139535959 17592186045417 true] [17592186220936 1075 17592186548667 13194139535959 17592186045417 true] [17592186220936 1078 17592186548667 13194139535959 17592186045417 true] [17592186222042 1070 17592186045485 13194139535959 17592186045417 true] [17592186222042 1071 "Given that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L\\{{t}^{n}\\} & =\\frac{n!}{{s}^{n+1}}, &  & s>0,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nuse linearity of Laplace transforms and the first shifting theorem to find the Laplace transform of  $f(t)=5t+3{t}^{2}{e}^{3t}$ for $s>3.$" 13194139535959 17592186045417 true] [17592186222042 1071 "Given that\n$L{t^{n}}|= \\frac{n!}{s^{n + 1}}, \\mid s > 0$,\nuse linearity of Laplace transforms and the first shifting theorem to find the Laplace transform of $f(t) = 5t + 3t^{2}e^{3t}$ for $s > 3$." 13194139535959 17592186045417 false] [17592186222042 1074 "First, we apply the linearity properties of Laplace transforms:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L\\{f(t)\\} & =L\\{5t+3{t}^{2}{e}^{3t}\\} \\\\  & =L\\{5t\\}+L\\{3{t}^{2}{e}^{3t}\\} \\\\  & =5L\\{t\\}+3L\\{{t}^{2}{e}^{3t}\\}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe are given that, for $s>0,$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L\\{{t}^{n}\\} & =\\frac{n!}{{s}^{n+1}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L\\{t\\} & =\\frac{1}{{s}^{2}} \\\\ L\\{{t}^{2}\\} & =\\frac{2}{{s}^{3}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe first shifting theorem states that if $L\\{f(t)\\}=F(s),$ then $L\\{{e}^{at}f(t)\\}=F(s-a).$ Therefore,\n\n$$\nL\\{{t}^{2}{e}^{3t}\\}=\\frac{2}{(s-3)^{3}}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the Laplace transform of $f(t)$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L\\{f(t)\\} & =5L\\{t\\}+3L\\{{t}^{2}{e}^{3t}\\} \\\\  & =5\\cdot \\frac{1}{{s}^{2}}+3\\cdot \\frac{2}{(s-3)^{3}} \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{{s}^{2}}+\\frac{6}{(s-3)^{3}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis transform is valid for $s>3.$" 13194139535959 17592186045417 true] [17592186233100 1068 17592186548904 13194139535959 17592186045417 true] [17592186233100 1071 "Find the equation of the tangent to the curve $f(x)=\\operatorname{ln}(4-2x)$ at the point $(1,\\operatorname{ln}2).$" 13194139535959 17592186045417 false] [17592186233100 1071 "Find the equation of the tangent to the curve $f(x)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(4-2x)$ at the point $(1,\\operatorname{ln}⁡2).$" 13194139535959 17592186045417 true] [17592186233100 1074 "First, we express $f(x)$ as the composite function\n\n$$\nf=\\operatorname{ln}u,\\,u=4-2x.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}u}=\\frac{1}{u},\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=-2.\n$$\n\nWe now apply the chain rule, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}u}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{u}\\cdot (-2) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4-2x}\\cdot (-2) \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{4-2x} \\\\  & =-\\frac{\\cancel{2}}{\\cancel{2}\\cdot (2-x)} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2-x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the slope $m$ of the tangent line at the point where $x=1$ is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x}|}_{x=1} & ={-\\frac{1}{2-x}|}_{x=1}=-\\frac{1}{2-1}=-1\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, the slope of the tangent line at the point $(1,\\operatorname{ln}2)$ is $m=-1.$ Now, we find the equation of the tangent at that point:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-{y}_{1} & =m(x-{x}_{1}) \\\\ y-\\operatorname{ln}2 & =-1(x-1) \\\\ y & =-x+1+\\operatorname{ln}2\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535959 17592186045417 false] [17592186233100 1074 "First, we express $f(x)$ as the composite function\n\n$$\nf=\\operatorname{ln}⁡u,\\,u=4-2x.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}u}=\\frac{1}{u},\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=-2.\n$$\n\nWe now apply the chain rule, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}u}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{u}\\cdot (-2) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4-2x}\\cdot (-2) \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{4-2x} \\\\  & =-\\frac{\\cancel{2}}{\\cancel{2}\\cdot (2-x)} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2-x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the slope $m$ of the tangent line at the point where $x=1$ is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x}|}_{x=1} & ={-\\frac{1}{2-x}|}_{x=1}=-\\frac{1}{2-1}=-1\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, the slope of the tangent line at the point $(1,\\operatorname{ln}⁡2)$ is $m=-1.$ Now, we find the equation of the tangent at that point:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-{y}_{1} & =m(x-{x}_{1}) \\\\ y-\\operatorname{ln}⁡2 & =-1(x-1) \\\\ y & =-x+1+\\operatorname{ln}⁡2\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535959 17592186045417 true] [17592186233301 1068 17592186548871 13194139535959 17592186045417 true] [17592186244257 1074 "Let $C$ be a positively oriented, piecewise-smooth, simple closed curve in the plane, and let $D$ be the region bounded by $C.$ If $P$ and $Q$ have continuous first partial derivatives on an open region containing $D$, then Green's theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}P\\,dx+Q\\,dy={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nThe region $D$ and the curve $C$ for this case are shown below.\n\n \n\n![](images/e3/e32b8e7d6ce80d7cedad928f9a2f7acc3c3d7f06add7909e856db27d154068d6.png)\n\nFirst, notice that we can rewrite our integral as follows:\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot dr={∮}_{C}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2+x)-{y}^{2})\\,dx+({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})\\,dy\n$$\n\nWe have that $P(x,y)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2+x)-{y}^{2}$ and $Q(x,y)={x}^{2}+{y}^{2}.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=-2y,\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=2x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can express the region $D$ as a type I region as follows:\n\n$$\nD=\\{(x,y):-1\\le x\\le 2,\\,{x}^{2}\\le y\\le x+2\\}\n$$\n\nTherefore, applying Green's Theorem, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{C}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1+x)-{y}^{2})\\,dx+({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})\\,dy & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(2x+2y)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{2}{\\int }_{{x}^{2}}^{x+2}2(x+y)\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535959 17592186045417 true] [17592186244257 1074 "Let $C$ be a positively oriented, piecewise-smooth, simple closed curve in the plane, and let $D$ be the region bounded by $C.$ If $P$ and $Q$ have continuous first partial derivatives on an open region containing $D$, then Green's theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}P\\,dx+Q\\,dy={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nThe region $D$ and the curve $C$ for this case are shown below.\n\n \n\n![](images/e3/e32b8e7d6ce80d7cedad928f9a2f7acc3c3d7f06add7909e856db27d154068d6.png)\n\nFirst, notice that we can rewrite our integral as follows:\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot dr={∮}_{C}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2+x)-{y}^{2})\\,dx+({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})\\,dy\n$$\n\nWe have that $P(x,y)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2+x)-{y}^{2}$ and $Q(x,y)={x}^{2}+{y}^{2}.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=-2y,\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=2x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can express the region $D$ as a type I region as follows:\n\n$$\nD={(x,y):-1\\le x\\le 2,\\,{x}^{2}\\le y\\le x+2}\n$$\n\nTherefore, applying Green's Theorem, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{C}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1+x)-{y}^{2})\\,dx+({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})\\,dy & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(2x+2y)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{2}{\\int }_{{x}^{2}}^{x+2}2(x+y)\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535959 17592186045417 false] [17592186250364 1074 "First, we isolate the exponential term:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{2}(0.43)^{4x+5}+4 & >-2 \\\\ \\frac{3}{2}(0.43)^{4x+5} & >-6 \\\\ (0.43)^{4x+5} & >-4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, recall that a positive number raised to any real power is always positive. Since $0.43>0,$ we have\n\n$$\n(0.43)^{4x+5}>0\n$$\n\nfor every real value of $x.$\n\nIn particular, every value of $(0.43)^{4x+5}$ is greater than $-4.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n(0.43)^{4x+5}>-4\n$$\n\nfor all real values of $x.$\n\nHence, the original inequality is satisfied for all real numbers." 13194139535959 17592186045417 true] [17592186250364 1074 "Isolating the exponential term, we find\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{2}(0.43)^{4x+5}+4 & >-2 \\\\ \\frac{3}{2}(0.43)^{4x+5} & >-6 \\\\ (0.43)^{4x+5} & >-4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBecause a power of a positive number is always greater than $0,$ we must have\n\n$$\n(0.43)^{4x+5}>0\n$$\n\nfor all values of $x.$\n\nTherefore, the inequality $(0.43)^{4x+5}>-4$ is satisfied for all real values of $x.$" 13194139535959 17592186045417 false] [17592186281291 1071 "Evaluate the mixed integral\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}[{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{1-{x}^{2}}}6y\\,\\text{d}y]\\text{d}A\n$$\n\ngiven that the region $D$ in the $xz$-plane is given by\n\n$$\nD=\\{(x,z)\\,:\\,0\\le z\\le 1-x,\\,0\\le x\\le 1\\}.\n$$" 13194139535959 17592186045417 true] [17592186281291 1071 "Evaluate the mixed integral\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}[{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{1-{x}^{2}}}6y\\,\\text{d}y]\\text{d}A\n$$\n\ngiven that the region $D$ in the $xz$-plane is given by\n\n$$\nD={(x,z)\\,:\\,0\\le z\\le 1-x,\\,0\\le x\\le 1}.\n$$" 13194139535959 17592186045417 false] [17592186281319 1071 "Evaluate the triple integral \n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}4x\\,dV\n$$\n\nwhere the region $R$ is given by\n\n$$\nR=\\{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,(x,z)\\in D,\\,{x}^{2}\\le y\\le 1\\},\n$$\n\nand $D$ is a finite region in $xz$-plane enclosed between the $x$-axis, the $z$-axis, and the curve $z+{x}^{2}=1$ in the first quadrant." 13194139535959 17592186045417 true] [17592186281319 1071 "Evaluate the triple integral \n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}4x\\,dV\n$$\n\nwhere the region $R$ is given by\n\n$$\nR={(x,y,z)\\,:\\,(x,z)\\in D,\\,{x}^{2}\\le y\\le 1},\n$$\n\nand $D$ is a finite region in $xz$-plane enclosed between the $x$-axis, the $z$-axis, and the curve $z+{x}^{2}=1$ in the first quadrant." 13194139535959 17592186045417 false] [17592186293989 1075 17592186548640 13194139535959 17592186045417 true] [17592186293989 1078 17592186293846 13194139535959 17592186045417 false] [17592186293989 1078 17592186548640 13194139535959 17592186045417 true] [17592186294072 1075 17592186548438 13194139535959 17592186045417 true] [17592186294072 1078 17592186293771 13194139535959 17592186045417 false] [17592186294072 1078 17592186548438 13194139535959 17592186045417 true] [17592186315030 1074 "If $F(x)$ is defined on $x\\in [0,\\pi ),$ the odd extension of $F$ is the function $f$ such that\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}F(x),